WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.340 --> 00:00:06.940
Health Dividend Six thousand six hundred and fifty diseases have zero approved treatments.

2
00:00:07.700 --> 00:00:11.920
At your current pace, you'll get to all of them in four hundred and forty-three years.

3
00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:21.080
Redirect one percent of military spending and it drops to thirty-six years, preventing ten point seven billion deaths at

4
00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:27.200
fifty thousand three hundred times the cost-effectiveness of anti-malaria bed nets.

5
00:00:27.920 --> 00:00:33.560
The whole thing costs less per year to run than Halloween costumes for dogs.

6
00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:40.400
But sure, take your time. That was the peace dividend: the money you get from buying one percent fewer explosions.

7
00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:45.730
Now here is the health dividend: what happens when you spend it on medicine instead.

8
00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:53.700
Right now, somewhere in your regulatory system, a drug that already passed safety testing is sitting in a filing cabinet

9
00:00:53.700 --> 00:01:00.460
while the disease it treats keeps killing people. Somewhere else, a researcher with a promising compound for one of six

10
00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:06.700
thousand six hundred and fifty untreated diseases is filling out the same form for the fourth time.

11
00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:11.180
The health dividend is what happens when you stop doing this.

12
00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:17.720
It is worth eighty-four point eight quadrillion dollars. That number is not a typo.

13
00:01:18.460 --> 00:01:23.660
The rest of this chapter is the receipt. From Explosions to Trial Slots.

14
00:01:25.180 --> 00:01:28.850
Twenty-seven point two billion dollars arrives from the peace dividend.

15
00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:35.880
Investor and political allocations take their cut, leaving twenty-one point eight billion dollars for medical research (eighty

16
00:01:35.880 --> 00:01:41.160
percent of the total). Your decentralized F.D.A. takes forty million dollars to keep the lights on.

17
00:01:41.720 --> 00:01:45.620
That leaves twenty-one point seven billion dollars for trial subsidies.

18
00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:53.200
Here is where the economics get interesting. Your current system charges forty-one thousand dollars per patient in a clinical

19
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:57.080
trial. Pragmatic trials, the kind your decentralized F.D.A.

20
00:01:57.240 --> 00:02:05.260
runs, cost nine hundred and twenty-nine dollars. At that price, twenty-one point seven billion dollars buys you twenty-three

21
00:02:05.260 --> 00:02:13.300
point four million patients per year trial slots per year. Your species currently enrolls one point nine million patients

22
00:02:13.300 --> 00:02:20.460
per year in clinical trials globally. The treaty adds twelve point three times more capacity on top of that.

23
00:02:21.260 --> 00:02:24.180
Remember this number. Everything that follows depends on it.

24
00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:34.400
The Waiting List. You met the queue in the untapped therapeutic frontier: six thousand six hundred and fifty diseases with

25
00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:39.020
zero approved treatments, and fifteen getting their first treatment per year.

26
00:02:39.560 --> 00:02:43.800
At that rate, you clear the backlog in four hundred and forty-three years.

27
00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:51.780
For context, four hundred and forty-three years ago, Shakespeare was writing plays, Galileo was building telescopes, and

28
00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:58.300
your species already had printing presses, universities, and intercontinental shipping.

29
00:02:58.300 --> 00:03:05.580
You just had not gotten around to curing most diseases. At this pace, you still will not for another four hundred and forty-three

30
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:13.010
years. Apply the twelve point three times multiplier. Instead of fifteen diseases getting their first treatment per year,

31
00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:19.960
you get one hundred and eighty-five diseases per year. The queue clears in thirty-six years instead of four hundred and

32
00:03:19.960 --> 00:03:26.800
forty-three years. The average untreated disease gets its first treatment two hundred and four years earlier.

33
00:03:27.660 --> 00:03:34.260
Your species figured out how to split atoms in six years during a war, but letting a cancer patient take a pill that passed

34
00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:38.980
safety testing requires the gestation period of three consecutive elephants.

35
00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.480
The twelve point three times multiplier compresses the elephant parade.

36
00:03:44.360 --> 00:03:52.260
But trial capacity is only half the delay. Once a drug passes safety testing, your regulatory system sits on it for another

37
00:03:52.260 --> 00:03:59.340
eight point two years to confirm it works well enough, while people who could be taking it die instead.

38
00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:08.340
Add the capacity acceleration and the efficacy lag together: two hundred and twelve years of dying, condensed into not dying.

39
00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.440
The Body Count. One hundred and fifty thousand deaths per day die from disease every day.

40
00:04:15.140 --> 00:04:22.580
Not all of those deaths are currently preventable, but ninety-two point six percent are *eventually* avoidable with sufficient

41
00:04:22.580 --> 00:04:28.060
biomedical research. "Eventually avoidable" does not mean "currently preventable."

42
00:04:28.460 --> 00:04:33.040
It means: given enough research, these diseases have biological solutions.

43
00:04:33.680 --> 00:04:38.320
The only category excluded is accidents (falling off things, being hit by things).

44
00:04:39.240 --> 00:04:46.240
Every disease, including aging, is a biological process, and biological processes are, in principle, fixable.

45
00:04:47.060 --> 00:04:55.060
This is the theoretical ceiling, not a near-term promise. Over a two hundred and twelve-year timeline shift, the cumulative

46
00:04:55.060 --> 00:05:05.080
toll: ten point seven billion deaths. That is five hundred and sixty-five billion disability-adjusted life years, or D.A.L.Y.s,

47
00:05:05.220 --> 00:05:10.580
of healthy life, and one point nine three quadrillion hours of suffering eliminated.

48
00:05:11.060 --> 00:05:18.400
These are cumulative, one-time benefits from permanently accelerating the discovery pipeline, the same methodology used

49
00:05:18.400 --> 00:05:22.920
to value smallpox eradication. The Price Tag on Not Being Dead.

50
00:05:23.860 --> 00:05:30.920
Your governments already put a price on human life when deciding whether to approve drugs, build highways, or regulate pollution.

51
00:05:31.440 --> 00:05:36.240
The standard rate: one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per quality-adjusted life year.

52
00:05:36.760 --> 00:05:43.120
This is not something I invented. It is what the E.P.A., O.E.C.D., W.H.O., and N.I.C.E.

53
00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:49.640
already use. The world loses two point eight eight billion D.A.L.Y.s per year.

54
00:05:50.260 --> 00:05:54.660
Ninety-two point six percent are eventually avoidable with sufficient research.

55
00:05:55.240 --> 00:06:03.490
At one hundred and fifty thousand dollars per Q.A.L.Y., that is four hundred trillion dollars per year in welfare losses.

56
00:06:03.720 --> 00:06:09.780
Multiply by the two hundred and twelve-year timeline shift and you get the total economic value of the health dividend:

57
00:06:10.860 --> 00:06:16.420
eighty-four point eight quadrillion dollars. "But that is more than global G.D.P.!"

58
00:06:16.840 --> 00:06:22.080
Yes. G.D.P. (one hundred and fifteen trillion dollars) measures market transactions.

59
00:06:22.460 --> 00:06:29.460
It does not measure the value of not being dead, not being in pain, and not watching your children die of treatable diseases.

60
00:06:30.100 --> 00:06:36.020
The one hundred and fifty thousand dollar per Q.A.L.Y. figure is not something I invented.

61
00:06:36.460 --> 00:06:40.960
I just multiplied it by the number of sick people, which is also not my fault.

62
00:06:41.560 --> 00:06:47.780
The only debatable input is whether the timeline shift is really approximately two hundred and twelve years.

63
00:06:48.340 --> 00:06:55.340
See the full derivation for how that number is calculated, and the Monte Carlo simulations for what happens when every input

64
00:06:55.340 --> 00:07:01.960
varies. The Receipt. Your decentralized F.D.A. costs forty million dollars per year to operate.

65
00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:07.660
For reference, this is roughly what your species spends annually on Halloween costumes for dogs.

66
00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:14.880
Less than one fighter jet. Less than what FIFA spends on "hospitality" (your word for bribing officials in a building with

67
00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:22.940
chandeliers). A system that could test cures for seven thousand diseases costs less to run than the outfits you put on animals

68
00:07:22.940 --> 00:07:31.280
that do not want to wear them. The R and D savings alone are fifty-eight point six billion dollars per year, a six hundred

69
00:07:31.280 --> 00:07:35.800
and thirty-seven to one return without counting a single human life.

70
00:07:36.460 --> 00:07:43.900
Include the lives (which you would think someone would), and the treaty's total societal return hits eighty-four point eight

71
00:07:43.900 --> 00:07:51.160
million to one. The cost per year of healthy life saved: zero point eight four two dollars.

72
00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:59.820
Anti-malaria bed nets, the gold standard of cost-effective interventions, cost eighty-nine dollars per disability-adjusted

73
00:07:59.820 --> 00:08:08.760
life year, or D.A.L.Y. This system operates at fifty thousand three hundred times the cost-effectiveness, at vastly greater

74
00:08:08.760 --> 00:08:14.300
scale. This costs less per life-year and nobody has heard of it.

75
00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:22.580
The total campaign investment: one billion dollars. Even the worst case (everything goes wrong, adoption is terrible, the

76
00:08:22.580 --> 00:08:27.800
servers catch fire) still returns an estimated sixty-six to one.

77
00:08:28.380 --> 00:08:32.020
Your worst medical outcome is better than your best military one.

78
00:08:33.419 --> 00:08:39.860
Every day without this system costs one hundred and sixty-one million dollars in wasted trial spending alone.

79
00:08:40.580 --> 00:08:46.160
The regulatory lag is not a safety feature. It is a waiting room where people die.

80
00:08:46.580 --> 00:08:55.620
You just gave it a nicer name. For you personally: if diseases get cured even a few years earlier, the average human gains

81
00:08:55.620 --> 00:09:02.360
roughly fifty thousand extra hours of life. You spent seventeen minutes reading this chapter.

82
00:09:03.080 --> 00:09:10.020
That is a one hundred and seventy-six thousand times return on your time, which is more than your investment portfolio has

83
00:09:10.020 --> 00:09:19.860
ever achieved. See impact dot war on disease dot org for derivations, Monte Carlo simulations, and the comparative cost-effectiveness

84
00:09:19.860 --> 00:09:20.500
table.
